<h2>Sound and scripting</h2>
<p>Everything in the room can be grabbed, dropped, thrown, crushed or sliced, so rather than wiring sounds up one at a time, I wrote general-purpose scripts in Unity (C#):</p>
<ul class="points">
  <li><b>Impacts:</b> attach it to any object, choose its sound for each surface, and the harder it hits, the louder it is.</li>
  <li><b>Dragging:</b> while two objects touch, a looping scrape follows how fast one moves across the other.</li>
  <li><b>Dialogue:</b> Animal Crossing-style babble, a random syllable for each letter as the text types out.</li>
  <li><b>Menus:</b> hover and click sounds (with a controller too), volume sliders, and a splash screen I animated and scored, which you can skip with a smooth transition.</li>
</ul>
<p>With those in place, giving each new ingredient its sounds was just a matter of dropping them in: bones, crystals, popping stars, flaming dragon scales, fish, roots, books, jars, and a slime ball whose whirr came from a failed experiment (measuring the force on its springs) that turned out funnier than what I was going for. The knife is a cleaver on my own chopping board.</p>

